Yuku's parser turns JavaScript and TypeScript source into an [AST](/parser/ast/). It recovers from errors, so every parse returns the full tree alongside every diagnostic.

## [JavaScript]($section.id('javascript'))

```js
import { parse } from "yuku-parser";

const { program, comments, diagnostics } = parse("const x = 1 + 2;");
```

`parse` returns a `ParseResult`.

```ts
interface ParseResult {
  program: Program;          // the ESTree / TypeScript-ESTree AST
  comments: Comment[];       // every comment in source order
  tokens?: TokenList;        // with tokens: true
  diagnostics: Diagnostic[];
}
```

The AST is described in [AST](/parser/ast/#javascript), and the other fields in [Comments](/parser/ast/#comments), [Tokens](/parser/ast/#tokens), and [Diagnostics](#diagnostics).

## [Zig]($section.id('zig'))

```zig
const std = @import("std");
const parser = @import("parser");

pub fn main() !void {
    var tree = try parser.parse(std.heap.smp_allocator, "const x = 5;", .{});
    defer tree.deinit();

    for (tree.diagnostics.items) |d| {
        std.debug.print("{s}\n", .{d.message});
    }
}
```

`parse` returns a `Tree` holding the AST, diagnostics, and source metadata. The allocator passed to `parse` backs the tree's arena, so `tree.deinit()` frees everything at once.

The AST is a flat array of nodes referenced by integer index. `tree.root` is always a `program` node. Everything below it is a tagged union you `switch` on.

```zig
const program = tree.data(tree.root).program;

for (tree.extra(program.body)) |child_idx| {
    switch (tree.data(child_idx)) {
        .variable_declaration => |decl| {
            for (tree.extra(decl.declarators)) |d| {
                _ = d;
            }
        },
        .function => |func| {
            _ = func;
        },
        else => {},
    }
}
```

The four read primitives are `tree.data(idx)` for a node's payload, `tree.span(idx)` for its source range, `tree.extra(range)` for a child list, and `tree.string(handle)` for string content. See the [AST reference](/parser/ast/) for the full node catalog.

## [Options]($section.id('options'))

```js
parse(source, { lang: "tsx", sourceType: "module" });
```

```zig
var tree = try parser.parse(allocator, source, .{ .lang = .tsx, .source_type = .module });
```

| JavaScript       | Zig               | Default          | Description                                                                                                                                                                                      |
| ---------------- | ----------------- | ---------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| `sourceType`     | `source_type`     | `module`         | `script`, `module`, or `commonjs`. Module mode enables `import`/`export`, `import.meta`, top-level `await`, and strict mode. CommonJS mode allows top-level `return`, `new.target`, and `using`. |
| `lang`           | `lang`            | `js`             | `js`, `ts`, `jsx`, `tsx`, or `dts`, the syntax extensions to enable.                                                                                                                             |
| `preserveParens` | `preserve_parens` | `true`           | Keep `ParenthesizedExpression` nodes. When off, only the inner expression is kept.                                                                                                               |
| `attachComments` | `comments`        | `false`, `.flat` | Attach each comment to its host node. The Zig field also selects whether comments are collected at all. See [Comments](/parser/ast/#comments).                                                   |
| `tokens`         | `tokens`          | `false`          | Keep every token. See [Tokens](/parser/ast/#tokens).                                                                                                                                             |
| `semanticErrors` |                   | `false`          | Also report semantic errors. See [Semantic errors](#semantic-errors).                                                                                                                            |

`sourceType` and `lang` can be inferred from a file path. `.d.ts`, `.tsx`, `.ts`, and `.jsx` select their language, and `.cjs` and `.cts` select CommonJS.

```js
import { langFromPath, sourceTypeFromPath } from "yuku-parser";

langFromPath("app.tsx");        // "tsx"
sourceTypeFromPath("app.cjs");  // "commonjs"
```

```zig
var tree = try parser.parse(allocator, source, .{
    .source_type = .fromPath("app.cjs"), // .commonjs
    .lang = .fromPath("app.tsx"), // .tsx
});
```

## [Diagnostics]($section.id('diagnostics'))

Each diagnostic has a severity (`error`, `warning`, `hint`, or `info`), a message, a source range, optional labels pointing to related code, and optional help text. Every Yuku tool reports diagnostics in this shape.

```ts
interface Diagnostic {
  severity: "error" | "warning" | "hint" | "info";
  message: string;
  help: string | null;
  start: number; // UTF-16 offsets, like nodes
  end: number;
  labels: { start: number; end: number; message: string }[];
}
```

In Zig, the range is a byte `span`.

```zig
for (tree.diagnostics.items) |d| {
    std.debug.print("[{s}] {s} at {d}..{d}\n", .{
        d.severity.toString(), d.message, d.span.start, d.span.end,
    });

    for (d.labels) |label| {
        std.debug.print("  {d}..{d}: {s}\n", .{ label.span.start, label.span.end, label.message });
    }

    if (d.help) |help| {
        std.debug.print("  help: {s}\n", .{help});
    }
}
```

### [Semantic errors]($section.id('semantic-errors'))

The parser reports syntax errors. Errors that need scopes and bindings, such as duplicate `let` declarations, `break` outside a loop, and unresolved private fields, come from semantic analysis, a separate and cheap pass. Leave it off when a linter or type checker already validates the code.

```js
parse(`let x = 1; let x = 2;`, { semanticErrors: true }).diagnostics;
// includes "Identifier 'x' has already been declared"
```

In Zig, [`semantic.analyze`](/parser/semantic/#analyzing-a-tree) appends them to `tree.diagnostics`.

